620 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1579 0 R
/E (The core issue identified here is that the dataset only has a cross-sectional binary label \(vital_status: Alive vs. Dead\) with no duration variable at all \(no survival_months, no time of event, and no time of censoring\). Without an elapsed time, it is just standard binary classification data, not lifetime data.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
621 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 402 0 R
/E (Point 2: Temporal Exposure Bias)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
622 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1580 0 R
/E (Because the time metric is absent and follow-up horizons vary across calendar years \(2013 vs. 2023\), treating vital_status as a simple target creates confounding. A 2013 patient who is labeled "Dead" had a decade to experience the event, whereas a 2023 patient labeled "Alive" might die next month.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
623 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 402 0 R
/T (Why is this not a Lifetime data?)
/E (Why is this not a Lifetime data?)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
