>>
endobj
696 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 408 0 R
/E (In clinical surveillance registries \(like NCI SEER and NAACCR\), missing or irregular clinical data is rarely stored as a standard NULL or generic NaN.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
697 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 408 0 R
/E (Instead, registries record sentinel values: domain-specific numeric placeholders \(e.g., 999, 98\) or standardized strings \(e.g., 'Blank\(s\)', 'Unknown'\) that explicitly document why a clinical measurement or procedure was not recorded in the patient's chart.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
698 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 408 0 R
/E (Usually through structural changes, we do see columns becoming systematically blank or a patient being inelligible for lets say, radiation therapy. These systematic gaps/implausibility are shown through sentinel values)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
